editorial:
## Method and reasoning
For a path, either skip the newest position and keep the previous optimum, or take it and extend the optimum from two positions back. A cycle cannot use both endpoints. Solve the two paths obtained by excluding the first or last position and keep the better answer. A singleton can be taken in either version.

## Walk through the sample
On the line, positions 1, 3 and 5 give 7 + 4 + 6 = 17. The endpoints touch on the cycle. Excluding position 5 leaves 7 + 4 = 11, while excluding position 1 gives 4 + 6 = 10.

## Complexity
Time O(n), auxiliary space O(1) beyond input.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

long long best(const vector<int> &a, int l, int r) {
    long long two = 0, one = 0;
    for (int i = l; i < r; ++i) {
        long long next = max(one, two + a[i]);
        two = one;
        one = next;
    }
    return one;
}
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (int &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    long long line = best(a, 0, n);
    cout << line << ' ' << (n == 1 ? line : max(best(a, 1, n), best(a, 0, n - 1))) << '\n';
}
